   "source": [
    "We will then do the imports and load the dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 18,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from matplotlib.colors import LinearSegmentedColormap\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
    "from helpers import extract_part, clara_kmedoids\n",
    "\n",
    "data_with_labels = pd.read_csv(\"../data/tomato_with_targets.txt\", index_col=0)\n",
    "labels = ['species','position','tissue','root']\n",
    "data_original = data_with_labels.drop(columns = labels)\n",
    "scaler = StandardScaler().set_output(transform=\"default\")\n",
    "data = scaler.fit_transform(data_original)\n",
    "tissue_dict = {'floral': 'gold', 'leaf': 'chartreuse', 'root': 'gray', 'sdling': 'mediumseagreen', 'stem': 'darkgreen', 'veg': 'purple'}\n",
    "species_dict = {'penn': 'orange', 'M82': 'blue'}"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Knowledge-based feature aggregation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We used [**gene sets from Gene Ontology (GO)**](https://geneontology.org) already in the first notebook of this course for the CCA example. Here we will use these gene sets to demonstrate knowledge-based feature aggregation, the first type of feature aggregation approaches. Each gene set represents a **specific biological function** (e.g., photosynthesis). GO covers several thousands of functional terms, here we focus only on the most relevant high-level functions in plants as defined by GO slim, and specifically the subset which has annotations for tomato plants. \n",
    "\n",
    "**By summarizing the gene expression values of each high-level function into a single value, the expression profile of a data instance gets much more compact** and can be visualized more easily, e.g. by a heatmap. Additionally, the compact representation can be used in follow-up machine learning analyses.\n",
    "\n",
    "The code below performs the following main steps:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Aggregating**: From the given n x p profile matrix, create an n x l profile matrix using a given l x p set membership assignment (sets may be overlapping and not each of the p features has to appear in one of the l sets). The aggregation function can be adjusted according to the context, e.g. mean or median are popular choices.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Visualizing**: The n x l profile matrix will be drawn as a heatmap. The n instances are reordered according to attributes of interest, which will be indicated as colored sidebars for the heatmap rows. For the heatmap, the values of the profile matrix are translated to colors using a color map with predefined breaks, which will be plotted next to the actual heatmap."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 19,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 700x700 with 5 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "df = pd.DataFrame(data)\n",
    "df.columns = extract_part(data_original.columns, part=0)\n",
    "df.index = data_original.index\n",
    "\n",
    "# Aggregation\n",
    "rd = pd.read_csv(\"../data/goslim_to_genes.txt\", header=0, delimiter='\\t')\n",
    "\n",
    "def aggregate(i, f=np.mean):\n",
    "    s = rd.iloc[i, 1].split(',')\n",
    "    currdatraw = data[:, [df.columns.get_loc(col) for col in s if col in df.columns]]\n",
    "    return np.apply_along_axis(f, 1, currdatraw)\n",
    "\n",
    "newdat = np.array([aggregate(i) for i in range(len(rd))]).T\n",
    "\n",
    "# Adding of row annotation and reordering of rows\n",
    "newdat_df = pd.DataFrame(newdat, index=df.index, columns=rd.iloc[:, 0])\n",
    "anno_df_raw = pd.DataFrame({'species': [species_dict[extract_part([i], part=0)[0]] for i in df.index], 'tissue': [tissue_dict[extract_part([i], part=2)[0]] for i in df.index]}, index=df.index)\n",
    "to_order = anno_df_raw.apply(lambda x: f\"{x['tissue']}.{x['species']}\", axis=1)\n",
    "s = to_order.sort_values().index\n",
    "anno_df = anno_df_raw.loc[s, :]\n",
    "newdat_df = newdat_df.loc[s, :]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Visualization\n",
    "def create_heatmap(X, mybreaks=np.arange(-2, 2.01, 0.01)):\n",
    "    cmap = LinearSegmentedColormap.from_list('RdBu', sns.color_palette(\"RdBu_r\", n_colors=len(mybreaks)))\n",
    "    sns.clustermap(X, cmap=cmap, vmin=mybreaks[0], vmax=mybreaks[-1], row_cluster=False, col_cluster=False, row_colors=anno_df, figsize=(7,7))\n",
    "    \n",
    "create_heatmap(newdat_df)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Data-driven feature aggregation"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Even if we do not have any prior knowledge about how features should be grouped,  we can still perform **feature aggregation using the dataset itself**. A simple and effective strategy is to **identify clusters of similar features** directly from the data. Note that we transpose the data for that purpose because the items we want to cluster are the features (genes) and not the biological samples (plants).\n",
    "\n",
    "For illustration, we apply [**kMedoid clustering**](https://www.vldb.org/conf/1994/P144.PDF) to the genes. Like hierarchical clustering, which is commonly used in heatmap generation, kMedoid clustering is **based solely on pairwise distances**, making it applicable to a wide range of data types. However, there are key differences:\n",
    "\n",
    "- In **hierarchical clustering**, once two items are linked in a cluster, they always remain together.\n",
    "- In contrast, **kMedoid clustering iteratively revises cluster membership**  by **partitioning around medoids**, leading to more **homogeneous clusters**.\n",
    "- The **medoid** of a cluster is an **actual data item** (unlike the centroid in kMeans). It is the item with the **smallest total distance to all other members of the cluster**.\n",
    "\n",
    "Here, we set the **number of clusters** to 10 because this is an adequate dimensionality for the heatmap visualization we are aiming for. To efficiently handle the large number of items (genes), we use the [**CLARA** extension of the kMedoids algorithm](https://www.vldb.org/conf/1994/P144.PDF)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 20,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "\n"      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 700x700 with 5 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "clust, medoids = clara_kmedoids(data.T, n_clusters=10, random_state=65)\n",
    "uclust = np.unique(clust)\n",
    "\n",
    "def aggregate_cluster(i, f=np.mean):\n",
    "        wc = np.where(clust == uclust[i])[0]\n",
    "        return np.apply_along_axis(f, 1, data[:, wc])\n",
    "\n",
    "newdat2 = np.array([aggregate_cluster(i) for i in range(len(uclust))]).T\n",
    "newdat2_df = pd.DataFrame(newdat2, index=df.index, columns=[f\"C{uc}\" for uc in uclust])\n",
    "newdat2_df = newdat2_df.loc[s, :]\n",
    "create_heatmap(newdat2_df)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Task:\n",
    "1. Reorder the heatmap according to species. Is there an aggregated feature that separates species?\n",
    "2. What is the advantage of data-driven feature aggregation vs. knowledge-based feature aggregation? What is the disadvantage?"
   ]
  },
